  1083	# --- rail round-3 F1: the WHITELIST ---------------------------------------
  1084	# Is $1 byte-equal to a destination the (already validated) route table
  1085	# declares? rc 0 = yes.
  1086	#
  1087	# WHY A WHITELIST AND NOT ONE MORE SHAPE RULE: the delivered-template list
  1088	# reaches the generator through the ENVIRONMENT, and every previous cure of
  1089	# this class enumerated what is FORBIDDEN — absolute, `..`, backslash, now
  1090	# glob. Round 3 found the next shape (pathname expansion produced entries that
  1091	# were confined AND legitimate-looking), which is the signature of a denylist
  1092	# architecture regenerating. The table already answers the only question that
  1093	# matters — "may the framework own this destination at all?" — for six paths,
  1094	# and it is the single source of truth the whole wave is built on. Anything
  1095	# that is not a declared destination can never be baselined from the
  1096	# environment, whatever its shape. This is the "enumerate what is PROVEN safe"
  1097	# inversion PLAN-185 W0 arrived at after the same class regenerated three
  1098	# times under review.
  1099	#
  1100	# Fail-CLOSED by construction: no table (or an unreadable one) yields an empty
  1101	# destination list, so every entry is rejected. Ownership is under-claimed,
