   403	# --- PLAN-183 W5 (D3): the THIRD reader of the shared delivery-route table --
   404	# Defect D3: this generator resolved every framework path as "$root/$rel",
   405	# which assumes the SOURCE relpath equals the DESTINATION relpath. That is
   406	# false for every route install.sh delivers out of `templates/` — on the
   407	# upgrade path (FMS_HASH_ROOT=$SOURCE_DIR) `docs/BRANCH-PROTECTION.md` hashes
   408	# the ROOT HOMONYM (wrong bytes recorded as the framework baseline) and
   409	# `.github/*.template` hits the `continue` and vanishes from the baseline in
   410	# SILENCE. The route table is the one place that answers "which source is
   411	# this destination?"; a local branch here would re-open the class CLAUDE.md §4
   412	# closed twice already (PLAN-182 16 modules -> one resolver; PLAN-167
   413	# _ownership_verdict).
   414	#
   415	# Same table, same idiom as scripts/doctor.sh:_route_source and
   416	# scripts/tests/_parity_classify.py. bash 3.2 floor: no `declare -A`, so the
   417	# lookup is a linear scan over ~7 rows. Skip guards act on the FIRST FIELD
   418	# only (the ownership_table.tsv idiom).
   419	#
   420	# rc 0 = identity route, source relpath on stdout
   421	# rc 1 = no row for this destination (the normal case for every framework
   422	#        path that IS identity-mapped: callers keep today's behaviour)
   423	# rc 2 = RENDERED, or the row is malformed => there are no framework source
   424	#        bytes to hash. Fail-CLOSED (CLAUDE.md §4: fail-open on
   425	#        infrastructure, fail-closed on INPUT). `${_wbm_rs_transform:-}` is
   426	#        deliberately an unbraced-default-EMPTY: `:-identity` was the rail
   427	#        S325 fail-OPEN finding that let a truncated row be treated as
   428	#        copyable.
   429	#
   430	# --- rail round-6 F3: the table has ONE source — the running checkout ------
   431	# The table sits NEXT TO this library (scripts/). Both callers source it via
   432	# an absolute $SCRIPT_DIR (install.sh:251, upgrade.sh:95), so BASH_SOURCE is
   433	# absolute and the lookup never depends on the caller's cwd.
   434	#
   435	# It is resolved UNCONDITIONALLY here, which is the point: this assignment
   436	# CLOBBERS any inherited value, so the environment is not a channel into a
   437	# table that drives WRITES (upgrade delivers into $TARGET, doctor --repair
   438	# copies into it).
   439	#
   440	# WHAT ROUND 6 REMOVED AND WHY. Round 5 kept an environment override behind a
   441	# test switch (an opt-in variable plus a candidate path physically under
   442	# ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}). Both conditions are readable and writable by anyone who
   443	# can influence the environment of an upgrade — setting TMPDIR is the same
   444	# gesture as setting the table — so the pair was never a production trust
   445	# boundary; it was a fixture loader living inside a production entrypoint. The
   446	# cure is not a stronger switch (that class regenerates: see round 3 F1 and
   447	# PLAN-185 W0) but the REMOVAL of the mechanism. Fixtures are now exercised the
   448	# way a real run is: against a COPIED framework tree whose own
   449	# scripts/delivery-routes.tsv is the fixture — the copy's library reads the
   450	# copy's table because BASH_SOURCE says so.
   451	#
   452	# The variable is deliberately NOT named FMS_*: every FMS_* name in this file
   453	# is an input knob a caller may set (FMS_ROOT, FMS_HASH_ROOT, FMS_PRIOR_MANIFEST,
   454	# FMS_DELIVERED_TEMPLATES), and keeping that prefix would invite the next
   455	# author to re-open the channel. `_WBM_ROUTES_TSV` is internal state.
   456	#
   457	# ONE in-process re-point survives, by design and with exactly one production
   458	# user: upgrade.sh copies the table's BYTES to a snapshot before a `--pin`
   459	# checkout can move the source tree, then assigns this variable (rail round-2
   460	# F2). That is code running in the process after sourcing, not an environment
   461	# read; the oracles assert that no THIRD production assignment exists.
   462	_FMS_LIB_DIR="$( dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}" )"
   463	_WBM_ROUTES_TSV="$_FMS_LIB_DIR/delivery-routes.tsv"
   464	
   465	# --- rail round-1 F2: the table is UNTRUSTED INPUT -------------------------
   466	# The table is a FILE, and a file is input even when it ships with the code: a
   467	# partial checkout, a bad merge or a tampered tree all reach these fields, and
   468	# every field reaches a filesystem path. Round 6 removed the environment
   469	# channel (above); it did NOT make the CONTENT trusted — the validators below
   470	# are what a row has to satisfy, wherever the bytes came from.
   471	# MEASURED pre-cure, S327: a row with
   472	# `dest=../../outside/PWNED.md` was accepted rc=0 by both readers and
   473	# upgrade.sh's `_up_deliver_template` wrote 536 bytes to
   474	# `$TARGET/../../outside/PWNED.md` — a real write outside the requested
   475	# target, before any ownership gate. A row with `src=../../../../etc/passwd`
   476	# reached `cp "$SOURCE_DIR/$src"` the same way, delivering foreign bytes as
   477	# framework content.
   478	#
   479	# A relpath is VALID only when it is: non-empty; RELATIVE (no leading `/`);
   480	# free of any `..` segment (leading, embedded or trailing); free of a leading
   481	# `./`; free of `//`; free of backslashes; and free of whitespace and control
   482	# characters. The predicate ENUMERATES what is acceptable and rejects
   483	# everything else — the "declare the safe forms, not the unsafe ones"
   484	# architecture PLAN-185 W0 arrived at after the same class regenerated three
   485	# times under review.
   486	#
   487	# Deliberately NOT a realpath() check: the fields name paths that do not exist
   488	# yet (a fresh INSTALL destination), so lexical confinement is the only
   489	# property available at read time. The physical confinement assertion lives at
   490	# the write site in upgrade.sh (_up_tpl_confined), belt and braces.
   491	#
   492	# rail round-3 F1: GLOB METACHARACTERS are rejected too. MEASURED pre-cure
   493	# (S327): `FMS_DELIVERED_TEMPLATES='docs/*'` passed this predicate — the entry
   494	# was pathname-EXPANDED by the consumer before it ever got here, so what the
   495	# predicate saw was `docs/ACCELERATORS.md`, a perfectly confined relpath. 125
   496	# unrelated adopter files entered the baseline as framework-owned, which a
   497	# manifest-honouring uninstall deletes on a hash match. The expansion itself is
   498	# killed at the consumer (`set -f` + a quoted read loop), but a path that
   499	# CONTAINS a glob metacharacter is also not a path this framework ever ships,
   500	# so refusing it here is the second, order-independent wall.
   501	_wbm_route_relpath_ok() {
   502	  case "${1:-}" in
   503	    "")            return 1 ;;   # empty
   504	    /*)            return 1 ;;   # absolute
   505	    ./*)           return 1 ;;   # leading ./
   506	    ..|../*)       return 1 ;;   # leading ..
   507	    */../*)        return 1 ;;   # embedded ..
   508	    */..)          return 1 ;;   # trailing ..
   509	    *//*)          return 1 ;;   # empty segment
   510	    *\\*)          return 1 ;;   # backslash
   511	    *\**)          return 1 ;;   # glob metacharacter *
   512	    *\?*)          return 1 ;;   # glob metacharacter ?
   513	    *\[*)          return 1 ;;   # glob metacharacter [
   514	    *\]*)          return 1 ;;   # glob metacharacter ]
   515	    *[[:space:]]*) return 1 ;;   # whitespace (incl. tab/newline)
   516	    *[[:cntrl:]]*) return 1 ;;   # control characters
   517	  esac
   518	  return 0
   519	}
   520	
   521	# --- rail round-5 F1: the DELIVERY DOMAIN, fixed in CODE -------------------
   522	# The table says HOW to route; this says WHERE delivery may EVER write.
   523	#
   524	# WHY THIS EXISTS: `_wbm_route_dest_declared` (round 3) is a whitelist of
   525	# declared destinations, but it reads the SAME table it is meant to constrain —
   526	# so a well-formed hostile table simply declares its own destinations and the
   527	# whitelist agrees with it. MEASURED pre-cure (S327): a row
   528	# `.git/hooks/pre-commit <- scripts/install.sh, identity` passed every lexical
   529	# gate, kept `routes == rows`, was copied into the absent destination, was
   530	# recorded in the manifest, and the upgrade exited 0. A confined relative path
   531	# is not the same property as a path this framework is allowed to deliver.
   532	#
   533	# The domain is a CODE constant, unreachable from any input: destinations live
   534	# under `docs/` or `.github/` — the two trees this wave delivers, by design
   535	# (ADR-194 §1/§4) — and sources live under `templates/`. All six shipped routes
   536	# satisfy it; widening it is an ADR amendment, not a table edit.
   537	#
   538	# rail round-7 F1 — "under .github/" is NOT the property this wave needs, and
   539	# MEASURED pre-cure (S327) the whole tree was in domain: `.github/dependabot.yml`,
   540	# `.github/workflows/pwn.yml` and, worst, `.github/workflows/validate.yml`
   541	# ACCEPTED. That last one is the shipped `validate.yml.template` route with four
   542	# characters removed from its destination: delivery would then write a LIVE
   543	# workflow into the adopter, contradicting the table's own `note` ("the adopter
   544	# never gets a live workflow from install") while the upgrade exits 0.
   545	# So the domain enumerates the INERT FORMS instead of a subtree:
   546	#   docs/<name>.md                    — one segment, Markdown, inert by content;
   547	#   .github/CODEOWNERS                — the rendered route (not executable);
   548	#   .github/CODEOWNERS.template       — its mutually exclusive twin;
   549	#   .github/workflows/<name>.template — one segment under workflows/, and the
   550	#                                       `.template` suffix is what keeps GitHub
   551	#                                       Actions from ever loading the file.
   552	# Nothing else — no second segment, no other basename, no other extension.
   553	# This is the same inversion round 3 F1 and PLAN-185 W0 already paid for:
   554	# enumerate what is PROVEN safe, classify the rest as refused. Widening it is
   555	# an ADR-194 amendment, never a table edit.
   556	#
   557	# $1 = destination relpath. $2 = source relpath, OPTIONAL: the write site holds
   558	# only the destination, and passing an EMPTY $2 is a rejection (not a skip), so
   559	# the one-argument form is the only way to check the destination alone.
   560	# rc 0 = inside the domain.
   561	_wbm_route_domain_ok() {
   562	  case "${1:-}" in
   563	    docs/*)
   564	      _wbm_rd_leaf="${1#docs/}"
   565	      case "$_wbm_rd_leaf" in
   566	        ""|*/*) return 1 ;;   # empty, or more than one segment
   567	        .md)    return 1 ;;   # ".md" is an extension, not a name
   568	        *.md)   ;;
   569	        *)      return 1 ;;
   570	      esac
   571	      ;;
   572	    .github/CODEOWNERS|.github/CODEOWNERS.template) ;;
   573	    .github/workflows/*)
   574	      _wbm_rd_leaf="${1#.github/workflows/}"
   575	      case "$_wbm_rd_leaf" in
   576	        ""|*/*)     return 1 ;;   # empty, or nested under workflows/
   577	        .template)  return 1 ;;   # bare suffix, no workflow name
   578	        *.template) ;;
   579	        *)          return 1 ;;   # anything GitHub Actions would LOAD
   580	      esac
   581	      ;;
   582	    *) return 1 ;;
   583	  esac
   584	  if [ "$#" -ge 2 ]; then
   585	    case "${2:-}" in
   586	      templates/?*) ;;
   587	      *) return 1 ;;
   588	    esac
   589	  fi
   590	  return 0
   591	}
   592	
   593	# --- rail round-7 F2: PHYSICAL confinement of the SOURCE --------------------
   594	# The destination side has had physical confinement since round 1 F2; the
   595	# SOURCE side had only the lexical predicate, and `[ -f ]`, `cp`, `cat` and
   596	# `sha256` all FOLLOW symlinks. MEASURED pre-cure (S327): with
   597	# `templates/docs/BRANCH-PROTECTION.md` a symlink to a regular file outside the
   598	# checkout, `[ -f ]` answered true and the delivered bytes hashed IDENTICAL to
   599	# the foreign file — foreign content installed into an adopter tree as
   600	# framework content, with the manifest then recording it as framework-owned.
   601	# A symlinked ANCESTOR (`templates/docs -> /elsewhere`) does the same thing and
   602	# passes every per-path lexical check.
   603	#
   604	# Two independent walls, both required:
   605	#   1. NO SYMLINK COMPONENT — every component of $2 under the physical root is
   606	#      tested with `-L`, leaf included. A real checkout of this framework has
   607	#      ZERO symlinks (measured: 0 under templates/, .claude/, docs/, and 0 in
   608	#      the whole tree excluding .git), so this costs production nothing.
   609	#   2. PHYSICAL CONTAINMENT — the deepest EXISTING ancestor of the source must
   610	#      resolve (cd -P/pwd -P; the bash 3.2 floor has no realpath) under the
   611	#      PHYSICALLY resolved root. "Deepest existing", not "the parent", so a
   612	#      source that is simply ABSENT — the `--pin` lane, where the destination
   613	#      list is this upgrader's and the sources are the pin's — still reaches
   614	#      its caller's `-f` test and keeps its "SKIPPED (source missing)" verdict
   615	#      instead of being renamed into a confinement refusal.
   616	#
   617	# $1 = source root (absolute). $2 = source relpath.
   618	# rc 0 = usable. rc 1 = refuse, with the reason in _WBM_SRC_CONFINE_WHY (a
   619	# refusal nobody can NAME is the silence D3 was made of).
   620	_WBM_SRC_CONFINE_WHY=""
   621	_wbm_source_confined() {
   622	  _WBM_SRC_CONFINE_WHY=""
   623	  _wbm_sc_root="${1:-}"
   624	  _wbm_sc_rel="${2:-}"
   625	  if [ -z "$_wbm_sc_root" ] || [ -z "$_wbm_sc_rel" ]; then
   626	    _WBM_SRC_CONFINE_WHY="empty source root or source relpath"
   627	    return 1
   628	  fi
   629	  if ! _wbm_route_relpath_ok "$_wbm_sc_rel"; then
   630	    _WBM_SRC_CONFINE_WHY="'$_wbm_sc_rel' is not a confined relative path"
   631	    return 1
   632	  fi
   633	  # `|| true` is load-bearing under `set -euo pipefail`: a failing `cd -P`
   634	  # would abort the caller instead of reaching the named refusal below.
   635	  _wbm_sc_phys="$( cd -P "$_wbm_sc_root" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P || true )"
   885	}
   886	
   887	# --- PLAN-185 W2: the GitHub handle grammar, with ONE owner -----------------
   888	# The SAME grammar upgrade.sh:3700 has enforced since PLAN-183 §9.2, expressed
   889	# once, here, where both executables can reach it. Its comment there says in as
   890	# many words that it exists so the upgrade cannot reproduce the 0-byte
   891	# CODEOWNERS defect — while install.sh, the script that PRODUCES the defect,
   892	# accepted the flag raw. A third implementation would have been the fourth
   893	# local branch of one shared value (debate C6).
   894	#
   895	# Equivalent to the Python `^[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,38})$`: 1-39
   896	# characters, first alphanumeric, rest alphanumeric or hyphen. The character
   897	# sets are ENUMERATED rather than written as `[A-Za-z0-9]` ranges because a
   898	# shell range is resolved through the locale's collating sequence, which in a
   899	# UTF-8 locale can admit characters Python's codepoint ranges never match — and
   900	# a grammar that answers differently in two locales is not one grammar.
   901	#
   902	# What the closed set buys, and it is the whole point: the value cannot contain
   903	# `/` (the sed delimiter that produced the 0-byte CODEOWNERS), `&` or `\` (sed
   904	# replacement metacharacters), a newline, whitespace, or a shell metacharacter.
   905	#
   906	# DELIBERATELY NARROW (OQ-2, Owner default): `org/team` is valid CODEOWNERS
   907	# syntax — the template renders `@{{OWNER_HANDLE}}`, so `@org/team` names a
   908	# TEAM — and this grammar rejects it. Supporting teams means changing the
   909	# delimiter in every consumer, which is its own wave; until then the refusal
   910	# says so instead of corrupting the file.
   911	# rc 0 = the value is a handle this framework may interpolate.
   912	#
   913	# rc.1 re-pass part 2 P2 — the predicate and the error text both call this a
   914	# GitHub-handle grammar, and it accepted `owner-` and `a--b`, which GitHub
   915	# does not issue. The installer then exited 0 after rendering that token into
   916	# every CODEOWNERS rule, so the repository carried the syntax of review
   917	# routing with none of the protection the success message implies. Two
   918	# refusals close the gap without renaming the contract: a final alphanumeric
   919	# character, and no consecutive hyphens. The consumers (install.sh's
   920	# `_assert_github_owner_grammar`, upgrade.sh's install-state reader,
   921	# doctor.sh) all pass through THIS function, so tightening it here tightens
   922	# them together — which is the reason it is one function and not three.
   923	_wbm_github_handle_ok() {
   924	  _wbm_gh_v="${1:-}"
   925	  [ -n "$_wbm_gh_v" ] || return 1
   926	  [ "${#_wbm_gh_v}" -le 39 ] || return 1
   927	  case "$_wbm_gh_v" in
   928	    [!ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789]*) return 1 ;;
   929	    *[!ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789-]*) return 1 ;;
   930	    *[!ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789]) return 1 ;;
   931	    *--*) return 1 ;;
   932	  esac
   933	  return 0
   934	}
   935	
   936	# One row's fields, validated together. Emits a breadcrumb NAMING the offending
   937	# row on stderr — a rejection nobody can see is the silence D3 was made of.
   938	# rc 0 = the row may be used; rc 1 = rejected.
   939	#
   940	# This is the ONE choke point every reader passes through (_wbm_route_src via
   941	# _wbm_route_meta, _wbm_route_dests, and therefore _wbm_route_dest_declared),
   942	# which is why the round-5 domain check lands here and not in each caller: a
   943	# rejected row drops out of _wbm_route_dests, `routes < rows` becomes true, and
   944	# upgrade.sh's AC-9 precondition turns the whole delivery into a named failure
   945	# with exit 3 (rounds 3/4 semantics) instead of a partial write.
   946	_wbm_route_row_ok() {
   947	  if ! _wbm_route_relpath_ok "${1:-}"; then
   948	    echo "    ERROR: delivery-route row REJECTED (invalid destination): '${1:-}' in $_WBM_ROUTES_TSV" >&2
   949	    return 1
   950	  fi
   951	  if ! _wbm_route_relpath_ok "${2:-}"; then
   952	    echo "    ERROR: delivery-route row REJECTED (invalid source '${2:-}') for destination '${1}' in $_WBM_ROUTES_TSV" >&2
   953	    return 1
   954	  fi
   955	  if ! _wbm_route_domain_ok "${1:-}" "${2:-}"; then
   956	    echo "    ERROR: delivery-route row REJECTED (outside delivery domain): '${1:-}' <- '${2:-}' in $_WBM_ROUTES_TSV — delivery writes ONLY docs/<name>.md, .github/CODEOWNERS[.template] or .github/workflows/<name>.template, from templates/" >&2
   957	    return 1
   958	  fi
   959	  return 0
   960	}
   961	
   962	# --- rail round-5 F4: ONE accessor for a row's metadata --------------------
   963	# Prints "<src><TAB><transform>" for the row naming destination $1, AFTER the
   964	# row has passed _wbm_route_row_ok.
   965	#   rc 0 = a valid row exists (its transform may be ANY declared value —
   966	#          judging the transform is the caller's job, reading it is not);
   967	#   rc 1 = no table, or no row for this destination;
   968	#   rc 2 = the row exists and was REJECTED (never collapse this into rc=1:
   969	#          rc=1 is answered by the callers' identity fallback "$root/$rel",
   970	#          which for a poisoned row hands back exactly the D3 behaviour).
   971	#
   972	# WHY: upgrade.sh's rendered-CODEOWNERS branch used to pull `src` and
   973	# `transform` out of the table with its own two `awk` calls — a FOURTH parser
   974	# of the shared table, which ADR-194 vetoes by name, and one that would not
   975	# have inherited the round-1/3/5 validators or the round-4 unterminated-row
   976	# fix. This is the accessor it calls instead. _wbm_route_src is now a thin
   977	# projection of the same row, so the file holds ONE loop over the table for
   978	# per-destination lookups, not two that can drift.
   979	#
   980	# rail round-4 F4 — `|| [ -n "$dest" ]`: a final row with NO trailing newline
   981	# fills the read variables but returns non-zero, so a bare `while read` DROPS
   982	# it. MEASURED pre-cure (S327) on a newline-stripped copy of the real table:
   983	# _wbm_route_dests emitted 5 of 6 destinations and _wbm_route_rows_total
   984	# counted 5 — the two agreed, so upgrade.sh's AC-9 precondition
   985	# (routes == rows) PASSED and the run shipped omitting the last delivery,
   986	# exit 0. A disagreement is visible; an agreed-upon undercount is not.
   987	# The row is still put through _wbm_route_row_ok: "process it" is not "trust
   988	# it", and a TRUNCATED final row (missing the transform field) still resolves
   989	# fail-closed at the caller, which sees an empty transform.
   990	# bash 3.2: `read` clears the variables on a true EOF, so the guard is false
   991	# on the next pass and the loop terminates (verified on 3.2.57 for the
   992	# unterminated, terminated and empty-file cases).
   993	# It ALSO publishes the two fields as _WBM_ROUTE_SRC / _WBM_ROUTE_TRANSFORM
   994	# (always reset at entry, filled only on rc=0). That is not decoration: it lets
   995	# a hot caller read them WITHOUT a command substitution. doctor.sh asks
   996	# _wbm_route_src once per manifest record — several hundred per run — and a
   997	# subshell per record is a fork per record for a linear scan of six rows.
   998	_wbm_route_meta() {
   999	  _wbm_rm_want="${1:-}"
  1000	  _wbm_rm_rc=1
  1001	  _WBM_ROUTE_SRC=""
  1002	  _WBM_ROUTE_TRANSFORM=""
  1003	  [ -n "$_wbm_rm_want" ] || return 1
  1004	  # rail round-6 F2 — the TABLE is validated before any ROW is consumed, and
  1005	  # an unusable table answers 2, never 1. rc=1 is the identity fallback
  1006	  # ("$root/$rel") every caller applies, which for a table nobody could parse
  1007	  # is defect D3/D4 arriving through a header instead of a wrong branch. This
  1008	  # also SUBSUMES the old `[ ! -f ]` check: a missing file is one of the ways
  1009	  # a table is unusable, and it now gets the same fail-closed answer.
  1010	  _wbm_route_table_gate || return 2
  1011	  while IFS="$( printf '\t' )" read -r _wbm_rm_dest _wbm_rm_src _wbm_rm_transform _wbm_rm_rest \
  1012	        || [ -n "${_wbm_rm_dest:-}" ]; do
  1013	    if [ -z "${_wbm_rm_dest:-}" ]; then continue; fi
  1014	    case "$_wbm_rm_dest" in \#*|dest) continue ;; esac
  1015	    if [ "$_wbm_rm_dest" != "$_wbm_rm_want" ]; then continue; fi
